 ],
 "literal": "They are foolish, may you live, the peacocks' dark flock! / Because seasonal rain poured, in response to that, / dancing they danced, and the piṭavu shrubs blossomed. / It is not the rainy season, O friend, let your affliction end! / The old water left behind by the passed rain, / in order to take in new water, shedding and bringing down, / having heard the thundering sound of the indifferent sky.",
 "translation": "May you live, my friend! The dark flock of peacocks are truly foolish. Mistaking an unseasonal shower for the onset of the monsoon, they have danced in response, and the piṭavu shrubs have burst into bloom. This is not the actual rainy season. Let your suffering end! They did this merely upon hearing the thundering voice of the indifferent sky, which is only shedding the old water left behind by past rains so that it can take in new water.",
 "modern": "தோழியே, நீ வாழ்க! மயில்களின் கரிய கூட்டம் அறிவில்லாதவை. பருவமழை பெய்துவிட்டது என்று எண்ணி, அதற்கு மாறாக அவை ஆடவும் ஆடின; பிடவ மரங்களும் மலர்ந்தன. தோழியே! இது கார் காலம் அன்று; உன்னுடைய துன்பம் நீங்குவதாக. கழிந்துபோன மழையினால் எஞ்சிய பழைய நீரை உதிர்த்துவிட்டுப் புதிய நீரைத் தம்மகத்தே கொள்வதற்காக, நமக்குத் தொடர்பில்லாத மேகம் இடிக்கும் இடிமுழக்கத்தைக் கேட்டுத்தான் இவை இவ்வாறு செய்கின்றன.",
 "explanation": "The speaker is the heroine's friend, addressing the heroine who is anxious about her husband's return. The husband had promised to return at the onset of the rainy season, which is the characteristic season of the Mullai landscape. Seeing the peacocks dance and the piṭavu shrubs bloom after a sudden shower, the heroine fears that the rainy season has arrived and her husband has failed to keep his word. The friend consoles her by explaining that this is not the true rainy season, but merely an unseasonal shower from an indifferent sky shedding its old water. She calls the peacocks and shrubs foolish for being deceived by the false alarm, thereby reassuring the heroine and easing her grief. The key words are 'kār aṉṟu' (it is not the rainy season) and 'maṭava' (foolish ones).",
